    "id": "c1",
    "text": "The Scorer’s Box\n\nThe summer I turned twelve, I became the scorer for my brother’s under 14 cricket team. The scorer’s box at Kooringal Oval was a tin shed with a sliding window and two stools. I would share it with Mrs Vu, who had scored for the club for nineteen years. I had watched a lot of cricket on television, so I was sure the job would be easy.\n\nMrs Vu handed me a pencil, not a pen. “Every ball goes in the book,” she said. “Watch the umpire’s arms. They tell you what to write.” A ball that rolled over the rope was worth four, she explained, and one that cleared it in the air was worth six. Most overs would be quiet, but a few would bring three things at once.\n\nFor the first hour, I kept up easily. Then a bowler began sending down wides, and the batters started running twos. I wrote faster and faster, and twice I looked at the wrong line. Mrs Vu said nothing. Now and then she tapped her own book with her pencil, which I took to mean that she wanted me to hurry up.\n\nAt the drinks break, the umpire came to the window and asked for the score. I read out 87. Mrs Vu read out 85, and my face went hot. She turned her book towards me and ran her finger down the last over, ball by ball, until she stopped at a four. In my book, it was a six.\n\n“That one fooled me too, my first year,” Mrs Vu said. I knew she was only being kind. Nobody who had scored for nineteen years could ever have mixed up a four and a six. I rubbed out my six and wrote a four, and the umpire wrote 85 on his card.\n\nIn the second half, I watched Mrs Vu more than the game. Whenever the umpire raised an arm, she tapped her book once before she wrote anything. It was not a signal to hurry. It was a signal to look up. After that, I looked up every time she tapped, and our totals matched at every break.\n\nAt the end of the season, I helped clear out the club cupboard and found a pile of old scorebooks. The oldest had Mrs Vu’s name on the cover. On its third page, a six had been rubbed out and a four written over the top. I never asked her about it. I just put the book back where I had found it.",
