    "text": "Steering Without a Wheel\n\nEvery March, the sky over Canberra fills with hot air balloons. For about a week, dozens of them lift off from the lawns near Lake Burley Griffin just after dawn, some shaped like teardrops and some like houses, animals or giant faces. Crowds come early to watch them rise. Very few of the people watching stop to wonder how a balloon with no engine and no steering wheel ends up where its pilot wants it to go.\n\nThe idea behind a hot air balloon is simple. Hot air is lighter than the cooler air around it, so it rises, and if enough of it is trapped inside a big enough bag, it can lift the bag, a basket and the people in it. A gas burner above the pilot’s head heats the air inside the balloon. To go up, the pilot fires the burner. To come down, the pilot lets the air cool, or opens a flap at the top to let some of it out.\n\nThe first hot air balloon to carry passengers rose from the grounds of a French palace in September 1783. Its passengers were a sheep, a duck and a rooster. Nobody knew whether living things could survive so high in the air, so the animals went first. They landed safely about eight minutes later. Two months after that, in November, two men made the first free flight by people, drifting across Paris for about 25 minutes.\n\nGoing up and coming down were solved in 1783. Going sideways is a different problem, and in a way it has never been solved at all. A balloon cannot push itself through the air the way a plane can. It travels inside the wind, at the same speed as the wind, which is why passengers often say the ride feels perfectly still. There is no breeze in your face, because you are moving with it.\n\nSo how do pilots choose where to go? They use height. The wind is often blowing in different directions at different heights: near the ground it might be moving north, while a few hundred metres up it might be moving east. By rising or sinking, a pilot can pick the layer of wind that is heading the right way. Before a flight, many pilots let go of a small helium balloon and watch which way it drifts as it climbs. This tells them what each layer of air is doing.\n\nThat is also why balloons fly so early. Just after sunrise, the air near the ground is usually cool and calm. As the sun warms the ground during the morning, warm air begins to rise in bumpy columns, and the winds grow stronger and harder to predict. A pilot who takes off at dawn has a few smooth hours before that happens.\n\nEven so, no pilot can promise where a flight will finish. Most flights end in a paddock or a park, with a chase car that has followed along the roads below. A good pilot can come down within a few hundred metres of the spot they planned. A great one, people in the sport like to say, plans to be surprised.",
