    "id": "lq1",
    "text": "The superb lyrebird lives in the damp forests of south eastern Australia, and it is famous for its voice. A male can copy almost any sound he hears: the calls of kookaburras and whipbirds, the creak of branches, even the click of a camera shutter. Some people have claimed that lyrebirds copy chainsaws, but scientists say recordings of this are rare and mostly come from birds kept in zoos.\n\nThe male does most of his singing in winter, which is the breeding season. He scratches together a low mound of soil on the forest floor and performs on top of it, spreading his long tail over his head like a silvery veil. A single display can last for twenty minutes.\n\nFemales are mimics too, though they are heard far less often than males. Recent studies suggest they use their copied calls mainly when they are defending their nests from other birds. This surprised researchers, who had believed only males sang. It also means that many early bird guides were wrong about the species.",
